<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tyburn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tyburn was a village west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, near today's Marble Arch, whose name became a byword for execution. From 1196 to 1783 it was the main place of public hanging for London and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a>, from 1571 using a large triangular gallows known as the Tyburn Tree. Among those executed there were Catholic martyrs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> era and the Irish lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-orourke">Brian O'Rourke</a> in 1591.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tyburn.jpg" alt="Tyburn" width="250" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Rocque The original uploader was ChrisO~enwiki at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tyburn_gallows_1746.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
